// The Darwin parking provider (runtime/park/darwin, #1403) against the real
// kernel.
//
// Darwin-only by construction, hence the sibling `darwin-only` marker file: the
// provider reaches libSystem through `extern function` (SPEC §11.7), which Bit's
// static ELF output cannot resolve and the compiler rejects outright (E0078) —
// the program is not merely expected to fail elsewhere, it cannot compile.
//
// The properties, the timing bounds, and — importantly — the limits of what can
// be proven without a second OS thread are all documented in ./../park/props.
// The one Darwin-specific note: `__ulock_wait` returns 0 both when it is woken
// and when its compare fails, so P2 rests on TIMING rather than on the return
// code. That is exactly why the shared properties assert elapsed time instead
// of trusting a status word.
//
// EVERY CALL IS HOISTED INTO A `let` RATHER THAN PLACED INSIDE THE `print`
// INTERPOLATION, and that is a bug workaround, not a style choice — see #1407.
// Calling a function that allocates from one interpolation slot corrupts every
// EARLIER slot in the same interpolation when the interpolated value derives
// from an `extern function` call: under BIT_GC=stress the earlier slots all
// render with the later call's value, and even a `bool` prints as `1`, so the
// slot's type is lost along with its value. This program is what found it (it
// is the first code to combine §11.7 externs with interpolation), and it must
// keep the workaround until #1407 lands or the suite goes red on Darwin for a
// reason that has nothing to do with parking.

import { parkProps, parkTotal, parkBackoffKat } from "../park/props"
import { parkWait, parkWake, parkSleepNs, parkMonoNs, parkCpuCount } from "../../../runtime/park/darwin"

function main() {
  let props = parkProps(parkWait, parkWake, parkSleepNs, parkMonoNs, parkCpuCount)
  let total = parkTotal()
  let kat = parkBackoffKat()
  let ok = props == total
  print("${ok} ${props} ${kat}\n")
}
